# Main-Line vs Side-Path

Do **not** treat a fixed list of paths or package names as gospel—the codebase grows. Classify **any** concern—`internal/services/*`, `pkg/*`, HTTP middleware, bootstrap wiring, metrics exporters, log shippers, etc.—as **main-line** or **side-path** using the criteria below. If classification is unclear or the change spans both, **ask the user** before reshaping APIs or wiring.

## How to classify

| | Main-line | Side-path |
|---|-----------|-----------|
| **Purpose** | Required for the core job users run bilirec for (capture, subscribe, room/session lifecycle, output path the product promises) | Optional observability, notifications, integrations, or extras that can be off with no loss of core behavior |
| **When off** | Product is broken or incomplete | Core recording/subscribe flow still works; users simply miss that extra |
| **Typical signals** | On the critical path of start/stop record, stream ingest, persistence users expect | Outbound HTTP hooks, metrics scrape, danmaku sidecar, remote log shipping, “nice to have” exporters |

Examples (illustrative only—not an exhaustive or frozen list): webhook-style emitters and Prometheus/metrics instrumentation often behave as side-path even when they live outside `internal/services`; recorder/subscribe/room logic often behaves as main-line. **Judge by role on the critical path**, not by directory or whether the code is structured as a “Service” type.

## Defaults (side-path only)

- Side-path features are **disabled by default** (empty config / false flag). Document defaults in `config.go`, `.env`, and Dockerfile per project sync rules.

## When disabled: no meaningful overhead

- No extra goroutines, HTTP clients, buffers, or per-tick work.
- Prefer a nil or zero-value service with `Enabled() bool` (or equivalent) checked **once** at the call site, or wiring only in `bootstrap` so main-line packages do not import heavy side-path deps when off.
- Avoid lazy init on hot paths for disabled features (no “first call allocates client” in recorder loops).

```go
// ❌ hot path always pays for an optional emitter
func onSegment(seg Segment, emit *Emitter) {
    emit.MaybeNotify(seg) // still builds payload, locks, etc.
}

// ✅ guard is O(1) and branch-predictable
if emit != nil && emit.Enabled() {
    emit.NotifySegment(seg)
}
```

## Do not change core structure for side paths

- Do **not** add parameters to **main-line** functions solely to support a side-path feature (metrics registry, webhook client, etc.).
- Do **not** thread optional callbacks through deep call stacks; emit from the edge (bootstrap, thin adapter file next to the owning package) or a single optional dependency at construction—not on every internal helper.

```go
// ❌ core signature polluted
func (r *Recorder) FinishSegment(seg Segment, hooks *SidePathHooks) error

// ✅ side path stays at boundary
// core file — no integration-specific imports
// adjacent adapter file — invokes side-path code only from known lifecycle points
```

New side-path work: isolate in its own package or file where practical, config gate in bootstrap, keep main-line packages free of integration-specific parameters unless the user confirms that integration is main-line.
